 \paragraph{Federated optimization and stationary-bias characterization.}
 \Scaf{} uses control variates to mitigate client drift caused by objective heterogeneity \citep{karimireddy2020scaffold}. Subsequent finite-time analyses refine the convergence theory of local-update methods. For example, \citet{luo2025revisiting} analyze LocalSGD and \Scaf{} under gradient and Hessian similarity, weak convexity, and higher-order smoothness conditions, while \citet{mangold2025sharper} establish sharper convergence guarantees for \Scaf{} on quadratic objectives.
+
+Stochastic local updates can introduce bias even without objective
+heterogeneity. \citet{glasgow2022sharp} study \emph{iterate bias}, the
+deviation of the expected SGD trajectory from deterministic gradient
+descent with the same initialization. Their finite-step bounds and
+continuous perspective connect this bias to nonlinearity, noise, and
+higher-order smoothness. Our quantity is instead the invariant-law global
+mean relative to the minimizer; we resolve its algorithm-specific
+second-order coefficients at a common stationary remainder precision.
 
 Beyond finite-time convergence, constant-step stochastic optimization can be studied through the stationary distribution of its underlying Markov chain \citep{dieuleveut2020bridging,chen2022stationary}. \citet{mangold2025fedavg} derive stationary bias and variance expansions for \FedAvg{}, while \citet{mangold2025scaffold} analyze the joint global-model/control process of \Scaf{}. Table~\ref{tab:prior-bias-resolution} compares the analytical resolution of these results with ours for the same algorithms: the updates and their stationary biases are unchanged, but the analyses differ in how precisely they characterize the bias beyond the shared leading term.
 
