+\subsubsection{Contraction cancellation and covariance-matched noise}
+\label{sec:num-mechanism-controls}
+
+Do the coefficients depend on the specific contraction $T[C]$, rather than
+on nonlinearity and noise alone? We use a nonquadratic trigonometric
+objective with $A=\operatorname{diag}(1,2)$ and $T\ne0$, and set
+$C_\rho=\operatorname{diag}(1+\rho,1-\rho)$ for
+$\rho\in\{-1/4,0,1/4\}$. All three covariances have trace two. At
+$\rho=0$, $T[C_0]=0$ although $B_{11}=(-0.0125,0)^\top\ne0$; the
+prediction is a zero client-independent second-order coefficient, not a
+zero raw mean. The two other covariances give opposite-signed control
+coefficients. At $\rho=1/4$, we also compare symmetric and asymmetric
+bounded noise laws with identical covariance and different third and
+fourth moments. Appendix~\ref{app:targeted-model} specifies the model and laws.
+
+We fix $H=4$ and use the Cartesian grid
+$N\in\{64,128\}$, $\gamma\in\{1/512,1/1024,1/2048\}$, with twelve
+independent matched pairs per configuration. Separate, unconstrained fits
+on $(1,\gamma,1/N)$ estimate the normalized coefficients in
+Equations~\eqref{eq:num-C} and~\eqref{eq:num-D}.
+Table~\ref{tab:mechanism-controls} shows the first-coordinate paired
+intercepts: the contraction null is separated from both signed controls,
+and the covariance-matched law contrast is small. For both coordinates,
+the null and law-contrast intervals lie within the prespecified
+coefficient-resolution margin $\pm0.004375$, equal to 20\% of the nonzero
+paired target magnitude. This is a fitted-coefficient resolution criterion,
+not a threshold for predictive performance.
