+\subsubsection{Trigonometric contraction and noise-law design}
+\label{app:targeted-model}
+
+For Section~\ref{sec:num-mechanism-controls}, let $\alpha=1/20$ and define
+\begin{equation}
+\label{eq:targeted-objective}
+\begin{aligned}
+f(x)&=\tfrac12(x_1^2+2x_2^2)+\alpha g(x),\\
+g(x)&=-4(\sin x_1-x_1)
+ +\sin(x_1+x_2)-(x_1+x_2)
+ +\sin(x_1-x_2)-(x_1-x_2).
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+The perturbation has Hessian norm at most $8\alpha=0.4$, so
+$0.6I\preceq\nabla^2f(x)\preceq2.4I$ globally. Derivatives of orders
+three to five are bounded, $x^\star=0$, and $A=\operatorname{diag}(1,2)$.
+For symmetric $M$, the nonzero third derivatives at the origin give
+\[
+T[M]=(2\alpha(M_{11}-M_{22}),-4\alpha M_{12})^\top.
+\]
+The covariances in Section~\ref{sec:num-mechanism-controls} yield
+$S_{C_\rho}=\operatorname{diag}((1+\rho)/2,(1-\rho)/4)$ and
+$T[C_\rho]=(\rho/5,0)^\top$. At $H=4$, the targets are
+\begin{equation}
+\label{eq:targeted-coefficients}
+\begin{aligned}
+ B_{11}(\rho)&=\left(-\frac{1+3\rho}{80},0\right)^\top,
+ &B_{20}^{\FedAvg}(4;\rho)&=\left(-\frac{3\rho}{20},0\right)^\top,\\
+ B_{20}^{\Scaf}(4;\rho)&=\left(-\frac{19\rho}{80},0\right)^\top,
+ &B_{20}^{\mathrm{ctrl}}(4;\rho)&=\left(-\frac{7\rho}{80},0\right)^\top.
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+
+Noise is $\varepsilon=C_\rho^{1/2}z$ with independent coordinates.
+The symmetric scalar law is $z_i=\pm1$ with equal probabilities; the
+asymmetric law assigns $\sqrt2$ and $-1/\sqrt2$ probabilities $1/3$ and
+$2/3$. Both have zero mean and unit variance. Their third moments are
+$0$ and $1/\sqrt2$, and fourth moments $1$ and $3/2$; all vector noises
+share the bound $\|\varepsilon\|\le\sqrt5$. Fresh blocks are independent,
+without sample recentering or rescaling. The theorem predicts common
+resolved coefficients at fixed $C$, not identical finite-step means:
+law differences may remain of order $\gamma^2/N+\gamma^3$.
+
+Each run uses $\tau=\lceil(0.6H\gamma)^{-1}\rceil$, $50\tau$ burn-in
+rounds, and 64 batches of $10\tau$ rounds. This schedule is heuristic.
+The grid lies below the imported threshold $\gamma_{\rm imp}=1/288$ in
+Equation~\eqref{eq:imported-step-threshold}; this does not certify every
+later absorption condition. The nonlinear balance observable in
+Equation~\eqref{eq:num-balance-estimator} is primary, with post-round
+global means as a same-run check. Noise is shared only within matched
+algorithm pairs; configurations, noise laws, and pilot and production
+runs use independent streams.
+
+The primary analysis uses the full grid and unweighted OLS with an
+intercept and regressors $(\gamma,1/N)$. A 4,000-draw bootstrap resamples
+run vectors independently within each grid cell, keeping algorithms,
+coordinates, and estimators together. This differs from reusing a seed
+label across the joint-grid path. A centered, standardized bootstrap
+maximum constructs approximate simultaneous intervals over 24 coefficient
+coordinates, six law contrasts, and six signed-covariance contrasts.
+Marginal intervals and Bonferroni-$t$ intervals with eleven degrees of
+freedom provide sensitivity checks. Sampling intervals exclude burn-in
+and deterministic grid error. Only two client counts are used, so these
+fits do not identify an inverse-client-count rate.
+
+The zero-target resolution margins are $0.011875$ for \Scaf{}, $0.0075$
+for \FedAvg{}, and $0.004375$ for their difference, each 20\% of its
+nonzero first-coordinate target. A comparison is resolved at that margin
+only when its entire interval lies inside the corresponding symmetric
+band; merely including zero is insufficient. The only restricted-grid
+analysis omits the largest step size and does not replace the primary fit.
 
